WEBVTT

00:00.448 --> 00:03.682
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:03.881 --> 00:07.149
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:07.184 --> 00:10.719
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:10.754 --> 00:13.722
♪ On which we used to rely? ♪

00:13.757 --> 00:17.225
♪ Lucky there's a family guy ♪

00:17.261 --> 00:20.562
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:20.598 --> 00:22.130
♪ All the things that make us ♪

00:22.166 --> 00:23.465
♪ Laugh and cry ♪

00:23.500 --> 00:25.005
♪ He's... a...
Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:35.212 --> 00:37.079
Ugh, look at Miss Tammy.

00:37.114 --> 00:38.513
What a skank.

00:38.549 --> 00:40.849
She's free-muffin it
at a baby school.

00:40.884 --> 00:42.985
Hi, Stewie.
Mommy's here to pick you up.

00:43.020 --> 00:44.920
Why do you feel
the need to narrate

00:44.955 --> 00:46.254
every little thing you do?

00:46.290 --> 00:47.823
Oh, hi, Mrs. Griffin.

00:47.858 --> 00:49.224
You know, I've
actually been meaning

00:49.259 --> 00:50.659
to talk to you
about Stewie.

00:50.694 --> 00:52.861
I'm concerned that he might
have an attention problem.

00:52.896 --> 00:54.396
Attention problem?

00:54.431 --> 00:57.199
Yes, he's been a little
difficult to deal with,

00:57.234 --> 01:00.335
<i>and on page two of three in the
How To Be a Teacher manual,</i>

01:00.371 --> 01:01.770
it says that any child

01:01.805 --> 01:03.872
who's being difficult
should be medicated.

01:03.907 --> 01:05.841
What?! Drugging a baby?

01:05.876 --> 01:07.075
Are you sure?

01:07.111 --> 01:08.610
That sounds so serious.

01:08.646 --> 01:11.313
I'm afraid his behavior has
been an issue for a while.

01:11.348 --> 01:13.882
He's even been bothering
students during nap time.

01:13.917 --> 01:16.518
Hey, you up?

01:16.553 --> 01:19.655
Guess where
I have a crayon.

01:23.460 --> 01:25.527
So, I spend, like,
$75 on cat toys,

01:25.562 --> 01:27.195
and what does she play with?

01:27.231 --> 01:28.830
A shoelace.
That's crazy.

01:28.866 --> 01:31.433
Joe, that's been your answer
every time I tell a story.

01:31.468 --> 01:32.901
It's like
you're not even listening.

01:32.936 --> 01:34.336
Well, that's 'cause
you interrupted me

01:34.371 --> 01:35.971
when I was telling
you about Kevin

01:36.006 --> 01:38.206
trying to hang himself
with an extension cord.

01:38.242 --> 01:40.008
I know, that's crazy.

01:40.044 --> 01:42.611
Hey, y'all want to try
some of our new food?

01:42.646 --> 01:45.480
I'm trying to turn this place
into a gastropub.

01:45.516 --> 01:49.251
These are Korean tacos, 'cause
whatever the hell nowadays.

01:50.621 --> 01:52.354
Ugh! These are terrible!

01:52.389 --> 01:53.622
(laughs)

01:53.657 --> 01:55.724
Quagmire,
when it comes to tacos,

01:55.759 --> 01:57.726
I'll trust the Koreans,
thank you.

01:57.761 --> 02:01.263
You're nuts. This tastes like
Ani DiFranco after a bike ride.

02:01.298 --> 02:04.132
Geez, Quagmire, since when are
you such an expert on food?

02:04.168 --> 02:06.468
For your information,
I'm an excellent cook.

02:06.503 --> 02:08.236
(laughs):
You? A cook?

02:08.238 --> 02:11.006
Huh. What's your specialty?
No-way soufflé?

02:11.041 --> 02:13.141
(laughs) I'm sorry,
that was out of line.

02:13.177 --> 02:15.744
<i>I am a good cook,
and I'll prove it to you!</i>

02:15.779 --> 02:17.879
Saturday night, you
and all your wives are coming

02:17.915 --> 02:19.181
to my place for dinner.

02:19.216 --> 02:20.415
Fine. We'll be there.

02:20.451 --> 02:21.850
Ooh, can we make it
Sunday?

02:21.885 --> 02:24.252
Saturday, I'm giving
Javier Bardem a haircut.

02:24.288 --> 02:25.954
So, what'll it be?
Everything.

02:25.989 --> 02:27.289
What do you mean?

02:27.324 --> 02:30.258
Long in the short places,
short in the long places.

02:30.294 --> 02:33.195
It should be from both
the future and the past.

02:33.230 --> 02:36.465
Something a child
would do to a doll.

02:40.170 --> 02:42.904
Sorry, I just prefer
to do number two at home.

02:42.940 --> 02:44.439
Where were we?

02:44.475 --> 02:45.907
It's Stewie,
Dr. Hartman.

02:45.943 --> 02:47.576
He's having trouble
focusing at school.

02:47.611 --> 02:50.445
Ah, yes. So you're telling me
your baby won't sit still

02:50.481 --> 02:52.380
in a way that's convenient
for his teacher?

02:52.416 --> 02:53.782
Yes.

02:53.817 --> 02:55.250
I'd heard stories,
but never thought

02:55.285 --> 02:56.918
I'd see it
with my own eyes.

02:56.954 --> 02:59.020
Mrs. Griffin, I'm going
to write Stewie a prescription

02:59.056 --> 03:00.722
for ADHD medication.

03:00.758 --> 03:02.724
Really? Well,
I don't know.

03:02.760 --> 03:04.893
Doesn't that stuff
make you wired?

03:04.928 --> 03:07.028
Well, if you're low-energy
and lethargic, yes.

03:07.064 --> 03:08.263
But if you're high energy,

03:08.298 --> 03:10.432
it actually calms you down
and helps you focus.

03:10.467 --> 03:12.534
At least that's what I'm reading
on WebMD.

03:12.569 --> 03:15.237
Wow, there are a lot
of different kinds of medicines.

03:15.272 --> 03:16.805
There's a cat in here.

03:16.840 --> 03:18.974
Okay, I guess we
can give it a try.

03:19.009 --> 03:20.342
Here you go.

03:20.377 --> 03:22.611
Now, who's this little guy?

03:27.151 --> 03:30.185
Wow, when did you learn
to cook, Glenn?

03:30.220 --> 03:31.820
Well, as a young man, I found

03:31.855 --> 03:34.623
that a great meal was the
quickest way to a woman's heart.

03:34.658 --> 03:36.057
How romantic.

03:36.093 --> 03:38.860
I mean, how do you think NBA
players get all those chicks?

03:38.896 --> 03:40.862
They're all great cooks.
Except Kobe Bryant.

03:40.898 --> 03:42.631
His-his... his secret
is different.

03:42.666 --> 03:44.099
Quagmire, you're really good.

03:44.134 --> 03:45.901
You could probably
make money at this.

03:45.936 --> 03:48.103
Yeah, maybe you could
open a restaurant.

03:48.138 --> 03:50.038
Or maybe get a cooking show.

03:50.073 --> 03:51.673
You know,
the 11:30 cooking show

03:51.708 --> 03:53.542
on Channel Five
needs a new host.

03:53.577 --> 03:55.210
The old one burned off
his eyebrows,

03:55.245 --> 03:57.012
and was just too weird
to look at.

03:57.047 --> 03:58.547
You should do
it, Quagmire!

03:58.582 --> 04:00.482
I'll even come along
and cheer you on!

04:00.517 --> 04:02.350
You'd do that for me?
Course I would.

04:02.386 --> 04:05.153
I love cheering people on,
like when I go to NASCAR.

04:05.189 --> 04:06.922
(crowd cheering)
Go in a circle!

04:06.957 --> 04:10.358
Go in a circle!
Go in a circle!

04:10.394 --> 04:13.061
Advertise stuff!
Advertise Stu...!

04:13.096 --> 04:15.063
Go in a circle!

04:20.037 --> 04:21.503
Hey, where's Stewie?

04:21.538 --> 04:23.724
Oh, he's probably
sleeping off his pills.

04:23.749 --> 04:24.308
What?

04:24.308 --> 04:26.608
Well, the school said
he had an attention problem,

04:26.643 --> 04:28.343
so Dr. Hartman gave him
a prescription.

04:28.378 --> 04:30.145
You're giving a
one-year-old drugs?!

04:30.180 --> 04:31.680
You're doping up a baby!

04:31.715 --> 04:33.181
Peter, are you
okay with this?

04:33.217 --> 04:34.549
Will you stop talking?

04:34.585 --> 04:37.552
I'm trying to think about a girl
I saw at the gas station.

04:37.588 --> 04:39.487
(door creaks)

04:39.523 --> 04:41.756
Stewie?

04:41.792 --> 04:45.560
(slowly):
Welcome to Pillow-World, Bri.

04:45.596 --> 04:47.896
Let me get up and greet you.

04:49.299 --> 04:50.966
There we go.

04:51.001 --> 04:52.367
How you doing?

04:52.402 --> 04:54.569
Oh, my God, Stewie,
you're totally drugged out!

04:54.605 --> 04:55.837
You know something?

04:55.873 --> 04:57.439
I have been thinking

04:57.474 --> 05:02.043
about how everyone in this
family, Brian, does their job.

05:02.079 --> 05:04.946
And they do a pretty
good job of it.

05:10.087 --> 05:12.087
God, I'm really freaking
out over this audition.

05:12.122 --> 05:13.655
What if I blow it?

05:13.690 --> 05:15.523
Quagmire, when I agreed
to come to this,

05:15.559 --> 05:18.326
I didn't know it would require
this level of emotional support.

05:18.362 --> 05:19.761
You Glenn Quagmire?

05:19.796 --> 05:21.630
I'm Carson, the
producer of the show.

05:21.665 --> 05:23.298
Peter Griffin.
I should be at work.

05:23.333 --> 05:24.733
Okay, let's see
what you've got.

05:24.768 --> 05:26.134
I think you're gonna like this.

05:26.169 --> 05:28.436
Cedar plank salmon
with a maple glaze

05:28.472 --> 05:31.973
and slow-roasted cherry tomatoes
on a bed of mesclun greens.

05:33.677 --> 05:35.243
Hmm. Not bad.

05:35.279 --> 05:38.880
Also, the tomatoes can be eyes,
and the lettuce can be hair.

05:38.916 --> 05:41.082
Look down.
There's also a carrot.

05:41.118 --> 05:42.951
(laughs) This is great!

05:42.986 --> 05:45.387
<i>Terrific food
and a hilarious sidekick?</i>

05:45.422 --> 05:46.655
This! This
is the show!

05:46.690 --> 05:47.822
What do you mean?

05:47.858 --> 05:49.257
I'm saying
you're hired!

05:49.293 --> 05:51.126
Just as long as your
friend joins you.

05:51.161 --> 05:52.494
What do you say, Peter?

05:52.529 --> 05:54.362
I say let's do it!

05:54.398 --> 05:55.897
Whoops. Uh, uh.

05:55.933 --> 05:57.299
Oh, I'm...
I'm sorry.

05:57.334 --> 05:59.668
What do I...
what-what do I do?

06:03.974 --> 06:07.108
I-I don't...
I don't know what to do now.

06:09.112 --> 06:11.146
That was the wrong choice,
Peter.

06:17.315 --> 06:18.814
Wow, Quagmire,
look at you!

06:18.850 --> 06:21.183
You're a real TV chef!
I know.

06:21.219 --> 06:23.786
It might sound silly,
but being a chef is something

06:23.821 --> 06:25.054
I've always dreamed about.

06:25.089 --> 06:27.623
I always dream about my
house filling up with water.

06:27.659 --> 06:30.626
That's crazy. But anyway, thanks
for encouraging me, Peter.

06:30.662 --> 06:32.128
DIRECTOR:
All right, places!

06:32.163 --> 06:35.264
We're rolling
in three, two, one.

06:35.300 --> 06:37.099
<i>Welcome back to
Quagmire's Kitchen.</i>

06:37.135 --> 06:39.235
Now, this mince pie
is about ready to go.

06:39.270 --> 06:40.803
Just needs a pinch
of cinnamon.

06:40.838 --> 06:43.739
And while we're at it, how about
we take the cinnamon challenge?

06:43.775 --> 06:45.408
Okay, Peter, we've all seen
the Internet videos.

06:45.443 --> 06:46.475
We're not doing that.

06:46.511 --> 06:47.944
I was talking about
something else.

06:47.979 --> 06:50.212
I was talking about a
serious cooking thing.

06:50.248 --> 06:52.448
Now, the ingredients
of a mince pie are traceable

06:52.483 --> 06:54.183
to the 13th century,

06:54.218 --> 06:56.319
when returning European
crusaders brought back

06:56.354 --> 06:58.354
Middle Eastern recipes
containing meats,

06:58.389 --> 07:00.890
fruits and--
oh, boy-- spices.

07:00.925 --> 07:02.658
Right here,
I'm using cloves.

07:02.694 --> 07:03.993
(coughing)

07:04.028 --> 07:06.095
Of course, most people associate
mince pie with Christmas.

07:06.130 --> 07:07.663
(hacking)
But I'll be honest.

07:07.699 --> 07:09.765
I'm not afraid of
a summer mince pie.

07:09.801 --> 07:11.867
I remember the first time
I had mince pie.

07:11.903 --> 07:13.135
Our neighbor, Mrs. Morrin,

07:13.171 --> 07:15.004
introduced it to me one afternoon.
Oh, God!

07:15.039 --> 07:16.339
I had house-sat for her
Aah!

07:16.374 --> 07:18.007
while she was visiting her aunt in Vernon
Oh, God.

07:18.042 --> 07:19.342
and she wanted to thank me.

07:19.377 --> 07:21.410
And, oh, boy,
what a treat it was.

07:21.446 --> 07:23.779
You done?
Yeah.

07:23.815 --> 07:26.682
All right, best two
out of three, cinnamon.

07:34.892 --> 07:36.859
Still on those meds, huh?

07:36.894 --> 07:41.130
Uh, yeah, they, uh...
they help me to, um...

07:41.165 --> 07:43.199
uh... think.

07:43.234 --> 07:46.302
Okay, time for
an eyes-open nap.

07:48.072 --> 07:50.306
God, look what those pills
are doing to you.

07:50.341 --> 07:52.575
Putting you on that stuff
was a huge mistake.

07:52.610 --> 07:55.211
Like inviting a turtle
to a cocktail party.

07:55.246 --> 07:58.648
Hey. Ask me why they
call me a box turtle.

07:58.683 --> 08:00.983
(loud munching)

08:01.019 --> 08:03.686
See, this is how
American society treats unique,

08:03.721 --> 08:05.287
enthusiastic children.

08:05.323 --> 08:07.323
Instead of embracing
their mental gifts,

08:07.358 --> 08:09.225
their first reaction
is to shoot them up

08:09.260 --> 08:11.627
with hard-core drugs
until they're mindless sheeple.

08:11.663 --> 08:13.596
All the better
to send off to Kuwait

08:13.631 --> 08:15.965
and feed the Halliburton
oil machine, right?!

08:16.000 --> 08:18.000
(scoffs):
Bush.

08:18.036 --> 08:20.836
Yup. Takes all kinds.

08:20.872 --> 08:23.639
You know, a lot of kids
at my school take ADD pills.

08:23.675 --> 08:25.808
They say if you're lazy
and unmotivated,

08:25.843 --> 08:28.177
it makes you super energized
and productive.

08:28.212 --> 08:31.080
Rea... Really? Productive?

08:31.115 --> 08:33.215
Yeah. And college kids
take it all the time

08:33.251 --> 08:34.884
so they can focus
on their studies

08:34.919 --> 08:36.719
and drinking alcohol
with their butts.

08:38.756 --> 08:40.723
Focus, huh?

08:40.758 --> 08:42.058
Okay, well, Stewie,

08:42.093 --> 08:44.794
these babies can help
the garbage disposal focus

08:44.829 --> 08:46.662
because that's where
they're going.

08:46.698 --> 08:47.997
No need to follow me,

08:48.032 --> 08:50.366
because I just said
what I was gonna do.

08:54.572 --> 08:56.605
My God, I can't stop!

08:56.641 --> 08:58.974
The words are
just pouring out of me!

08:59.010 --> 09:01.877
I have more energy than a guy
who caught a T-shirt!

09:01.913 --> 09:03.879
(crowd cheering)

09:03.915 --> 09:06.182
My wife can wear this to sleep!

09:06.217 --> 09:09.085
My wife can wear this
to sleep!

09:13.658 --> 09:16.225
Okay, today we're putting
together duck breast medallions

09:16.260 --> 09:17.827
with a port wine reduction.

09:17.862 --> 09:19.628
And as I'm walking you
through the steps,

09:19.664 --> 09:22.565
watch your screen for
Junior Chef Peter's pop-up tips:

09:22.600 --> 09:25.568
helpful hints and food factoids
to make your meal a success.

09:25.603 --> 09:27.036
Now, you're gonna want to start

09:27.071 --> 09:29.238
with a large,
self-sealing plastic bag.

09:29.273 --> 09:31.507
And in that bag,
we'll combine one large,

09:31.542 --> 09:33.642
finely-chopped garlic clove;

09:33.678 --> 09:36.245
one tablespoon of grated,
peeled fresh ginger;

09:36.280 --> 09:38.514
two teaspoons
of five-spice powder;

09:38.549 --> 09:41.784
a teaspoon of salt; and a half
teaspoon of fresh ground pepper.

09:41.819 --> 09:44.086
Then you're gonna want
to add the duck breasts,

09:44.122 --> 09:47.189
seal the bag, and refrigerate
that for at least one hour,

09:47.225 --> 09:50.192
or up to 24 hours, if you want
to prepare this ahead of time.

09:50.228 --> 09:52.561
Just make sure you remove
the bag from the refrigerator

09:52.597 --> 09:54.130
one hour before cooking.

09:54.165 --> 09:57.767
You're gonna put that in your
oven, preheated to 400 degrees.

09:59.637 --> 10:01.370
So we just spoon
on the drippings,

10:01.405 --> 10:03.105
and that'll brown
the skin nicely.

10:03.141 --> 10:07.476
<i>If you bang these metal spoons
on everything, it's like Stomp.</i>

10:07.512 --> 10:10.146
Look at me! I'm huge in 2002!

10:10.181 --> 10:11.647
Peter...

10:11.682 --> 10:14.483
Nothing like a night at the
theater that ends in a headache!

10:14.519 --> 10:15.985
Damn it, Peter,
that's it!

10:16.020 --> 10:18.454
I can't do this show with
you anymore! You're fired!

10:18.489 --> 10:19.755
What?! You can't fire me!

10:19.791 --> 10:21.557
Only Mr. Spacely can fire me.

10:21.592 --> 10:24.760
Griffin...
you're fired!

10:24.796 --> 10:27.463
(voice breaking):
Okay, that's fair.

10:27.498 --> 10:30.132
(crying)

10:31.869 --> 10:33.502
Peter, can
I come in?

10:33.538 --> 10:36.672
Hold on, let me put on
my weird old actress turban.

10:37.708 --> 10:39.375
Yes?

10:39.410 --> 10:41.811
Listen, I saw what happened,
but you should know,

10:41.846 --> 10:43.913
we at the station
would hate to lose you.

10:43.948 --> 10:45.447
You're a big hit
with the viewers.

10:45.483 --> 10:48.150
I will not work
with that man again.

10:48.186 --> 10:49.985
I'm not asking
you to. In fact,

10:50.021 --> 10:52.555
we'd like to offer you
your own cooking show.

10:52.590 --> 10:55.157
Hmm, let me consult
with my lawyer.

10:55.193 --> 10:57.660
Well, Peter, since the DUIs
happened so close together,

10:57.695 --> 10:59.662
we don't really have
much wiggle room here.

10:59.697 --> 11:02.865
I suggest you take the jail time
and just get it over with.

11:02.900 --> 11:05.835
I'll do it.
After October 13.

11:12.944 --> 11:14.443
Come on, Rupert.

11:14.478 --> 11:15.711
Wait-wait till you see this.

11:15.746 --> 11:18.180
This is really
something special.

11:18.216 --> 11:22.718
Check out how much water
is in the dehumidifier.

11:22.753 --> 11:25.321
Wow!

11:25.356 --> 11:27.690
That was all in the air.

11:27.725 --> 11:29.225
(light clattering)

11:30.928 --> 11:32.061
Brian?

11:32.096 --> 11:33.629
Is that... is that, uh...

11:33.664 --> 11:35.931
Is that, uh... you?

11:35.967 --> 11:37.333
Stewie! Rearrange.

11:37.368 --> 11:38.834
Ewiets. Rearrange.

11:38.870 --> 11:40.236
Wieest. Restore.

11:40.271 --> 11:42.271
Stewie. Hey, come see
what I've been working on.

11:44.108 --> 11:47.076
I call it
Space Shire Seven.

11:47.111 --> 11:48.711
Nice, nice.

11:48.746 --> 11:52.047
It's like, uh, it's like
a fake, uh, place you made?

11:52.083 --> 11:54.817
It's a medieval sci-fi universe,
adaptable to TV,

11:54.852 --> 11:56.485
movies, toys, games,
and mobile apps!

11:56.520 --> 11:58.554
I threw together a 2,000-page
treatment last night.

11:58.589 --> 12:00.256
And I just figured,
the hell with it,

12:00.291 --> 12:02.124
why not build a scale model
and record voices

12:02.159 --> 12:04.293
for all the characters? Watch.

12:04.328 --> 12:05.761
Well met, stranger.

12:05.796 --> 12:07.963
<i>Arasha deekaba plitcho.</i>

12:07.999 --> 12:09.832
That's a Nebulon Mage.
He speaks Ga'bi,

12:09.867 --> 12:11.667
this dumb language I wrote.

12:11.702 --> 12:13.736
It's then... illegal?

12:13.771 --> 12:15.738
What?

12:15.773 --> 12:17.773
Hello?

12:22.146 --> 12:24.380
Okay, now we add
the diced tomatoes.

12:24.415 --> 12:25.881
(thumping dance music,
applause in distance)

12:25.917 --> 12:27.082
Just gonna keep going here.

12:27.118 --> 12:28.951
I like to drizzle these
with a fig balsamic...

12:28.986 --> 12:30.386
(crowd cheers in distance)
Damn it.

12:30.421 --> 12:32.087
What the hell
is going on over there?!

12:33.858 --> 12:35.357
♪ I don't want ♪

12:35.393 --> 12:36.759
♪ A place to stay... ♪

12:36.794 --> 12:39.128
And that's my famous paella
that looks and tastes

12:39.163 --> 12:40.529
just like a pizza bagel.

12:40.564 --> 12:41.864
It's a pizza bagel.

12:41.899 --> 12:45.567
Now, as always,
add butter to taste.

12:45.603 --> 12:47.269
Mmm, delicious!

12:47.305 --> 12:49.305
Butter dunk!
(buzzer sounds)

12:49.340 --> 12:50.340
(crowd cheering)

12:52.543 --> 12:54.310
Stop it!
Stop the show!

12:54.345 --> 12:55.811
What the hell
are you doing?

12:55.846 --> 12:57.813
You're just pouring
melted butter onto frozen foods.

12:57.848 --> 12:59.315
You're not a chef.

12:59.350 --> 13:02.017
Oh, really? Then why do I
already have a restaurant

13:02.053 --> 13:04.720
in Orlando's largest
indoor water park?

13:04.755 --> 13:07.289
This is an insult to anyone
who cares about cooking.

13:07.325 --> 13:09.491
Either you leave
the network or I do!

13:09.527 --> 13:12.328
Guys, guys, look, I know how
we can settle this: a cook-off.

13:12.363 --> 13:13.862
Each of you
cooks a meal

13:13.898 --> 13:16.532
<i>using a secret ingredient,
Iron Chef style.</i>

13:16.567 --> 13:18.300
Will we be allowed
to use calculators?

13:18.336 --> 13:19.568
Um, I... I don't...

13:19.603 --> 13:20.836
What?

13:20.871 --> 13:22.338
I say bring it on!

13:22.373 --> 13:23.939
Fine! A cook-off it is.

13:23.975 --> 13:25.674
See you in the
kitchen, jerk.

13:25.710 --> 13:28.477
I'll see you
in hell, loser.

13:29.947 --> 13:31.947
Bad day to carpool.
Yeah.

13:36.934 --> 13:38.900
Okay, Joe, Peter and I
are going head-to-head

13:38.962 --> 13:40.128
in that cooking
competition,

13:40.163 --> 13:41.796
and they're letting us
each use an assistant.

13:41.831 --> 13:43.131
Will you help me out?

13:43.166 --> 13:44.999
Okay, but just promise me
there's no reading.

13:45.035 --> 13:46.200
I'm not a strong reader.

13:46.236 --> 13:47.435
What?

13:47.470 --> 13:48.736
I'm not illiterate,
I'm just slow.

13:48.772 --> 13:50.238
What are you talking about?

13:50.273 --> 13:53.808
I never tried hard in school
'cause I could always run fast.

13:55.445 --> 13:58.146
So you're cool with being
my chef helper for the big show?

13:58.181 --> 13:59.647
Sure, but I got
to be honest--

13:59.683 --> 14:01.115
if it ain't a
fish with whiskers,

14:01.151 --> 14:02.750
I don't know what
to do with it.

14:02.786 --> 14:03.985
But if it is?

14:04.020 --> 14:06.988
Then sweet sassy molassey!
(chuckles)

14:07.023 --> 14:09.724
Great. Don't talk that way
when we're on the show.

14:09.759 --> 14:11.726
♪ ♪

14:11.761 --> 14:13.728
♪ You're taking a chance ♪

14:13.763 --> 14:15.897
♪ Risking it all ♪

14:15.932 --> 14:19.233
♪ For the thrill of the moment ♪

14:19.269 --> 14:20.835
♪ Taking a stand ♪

14:20.870 --> 14:23.671
♪ You ain't gonna fall ♪

14:23.707 --> 14:26.107
♪ And you've always known it ♪

14:26.142 --> 14:29.911
♪ They're dying to shake you ♪

14:29.946 --> 14:32.547
♪ Trying their best
to break you ♪

14:32.582 --> 14:35.016
♪ And though
the going is rough ♪

14:35.051 --> 14:38.086
♪ You're going home as a hero ♪

14:39.422 --> 14:42.690
♪ 'Cause there's
thunder in your heart ♪

14:42.726 --> 14:46.461
♪ Every move is like lightning ♪

14:46.496 --> 14:48.329
♪ It's the power you feel ♪

14:48.364 --> 14:50.531
♪ When you get your taste ♪

14:50.567 --> 14:53.334
♪ Of the glory ♪

14:53.369 --> 14:55.870
♪ You can light the dark ♪

14:55.905 --> 14:59.874
♪ When they hear
your heart of thunder. ♪

15:04.180 --> 15:05.813
Stewie! Stew-ez Canal.

15:05.849 --> 15:07.849
You're here!
We got to get going.

15:07.884 --> 15:10.051
Going?

15:10.086 --> 15:11.252
Where?

15:11.287 --> 15:12.420
To Hartford, man.

15:12.455 --> 15:14.021
There's a book fair
at the convention center,

15:14.057 --> 15:15.656
and George R. R. Martin
will be there.

15:15.692 --> 15:17.158
Who-Who's that?

15:17.193 --> 15:19.727
<i>Uh, Game of Thrones?
Black shirts? Dandruff?</i>

15:19.763 --> 15:21.863
Takes down more nerd chotch
than anyone around?

15:21.898 --> 15:23.164
Okay.

15:23.199 --> 15:24.665
Anyway, I'm gonna
show him my models

15:24.701 --> 15:26.801
and pitch him
Space Shire Seven.

15:26.836 --> 15:30.638
Okay, before we go,
I should wet my eyes.

15:30.673 --> 15:32.673
(slow scraping)

15:37.614 --> 15:39.380
There.

15:41.885 --> 15:45.353
♪ ♪

15:45.388 --> 15:47.622
Good afternoon, I'm Tom Tucker,
and we have a fantastic

15:47.657 --> 15:50.992
cooking competition for you
today with two fantastic chefs.

15:51.027 --> 15:52.493
Really?

15:52.529 --> 15:54.662
You couldn't find another
adjective for "fantastic"?

15:54.697 --> 15:56.130
Used "fantastic" twice?
Who wrote this?

15:56.166 --> 15:57.498
DIRECTOR:
Erica did.

15:57.534 --> 15:59.667
Wait, is that the one
I called fat and we can't fire?

15:59.702 --> 16:00.868
Yeah.

16:00.904 --> 16:02.904
Let's get this
contest started!

16:05.842 --> 16:07.909
Big whoop. I can do that.

16:07.944 --> 16:09.177
(woman screams)

16:09.212 --> 16:10.444
DIRECTOR:
Oh, my God, Erica!

16:10.480 --> 16:11.646
You killed Erica!

16:11.681 --> 16:13.748
TOM:
Fantastic.

16:19.789 --> 16:22.089
That's him.
George R. R. Martin.

16:24.961 --> 16:26.627
God, he must be
the coolest fat guy

16:26.663 --> 16:28.663
in a train conductor's cap
in the world!

16:28.698 --> 16:30.531
Mr. Martin,
I'm Brian Griffin,

16:30.567 --> 16:32.834
and I've got a pitch
that's gonna blow you away.

16:32.869 --> 16:34.168
Are you sitting down?

16:34.204 --> 16:35.469
Almost always.

16:35.505 --> 16:37.705
Then join me
on an interstellar crusade

16:37.740 --> 16:40.775
to Space Shire Seven!

16:40.810 --> 16:43.077
Our story begins
50,000 fortnights ago,

16:43.112 --> 16:45.646
in the distant galaxy
of Warlock's Keep.

16:45.682 --> 16:47.181
Comet Castle falls.

16:47.217 --> 16:49.417
Samson Cyberblood is the new
king of the star system.

16:49.452 --> 16:51.419
Long live
Space Shire Seven,

16:51.454 --> 16:53.621
and long live
the Hyperspeed Throne!

16:53.656 --> 16:54.656
(panting)

16:55.692 --> 16:57.425
So? What do you think?

16:57.460 --> 16:58.659
It was terrible.

16:58.695 --> 16:59.894
Ah, you're
speaking the language

16:59.929 --> 17:01.329
of the Opposite Goblins.

17:01.364 --> 17:02.663
I'm very flattered.

17:02.699 --> 17:03.965
No, it's garbage.

17:04.000 --> 17:05.533
You just took a lot of pills,

17:05.568 --> 17:08.002
mashed together a bunch
of sci-fi and fantasy clichés

17:08.037 --> 17:11.105
and crapped out
a 2,000-page turd.

17:11.140 --> 17:13.241
Y-You could tell
I was on medication?

17:13.276 --> 17:16.077
Oh, sure. But that stuff
doesn't make you write well,

17:16.112 --> 17:17.879
it just makes you
write a lot.

17:17.914 --> 17:19.380
Now, if you'll excuse me,

17:19.415 --> 17:22.416
I have to go wash the nerd
chotch out of my beard.

17:25.088 --> 17:26.420
God. I didn't realize

17:26.456 --> 17:29.624
how those drugs were
ruining me creatively.

17:29.659 --> 17:32.193
I haven't been feeling
quite right either.

17:32.228 --> 17:35.463
Yesterday, I watched Chris
shower for half an hour

17:35.498 --> 17:38.065
just because
I thought it was the TV.

17:38.101 --> 17:39.533
He, um...

17:39.569 --> 17:41.469
he lies on his back
in there.

17:41.504 --> 17:43.838
Stewie, this drug
is ruining us.

17:43.873 --> 17:45.039
We don't need these.

17:45.074 --> 17:46.474
You're right.

17:46.509 --> 17:49.043
These pills are for people
with real problems.

17:49.078 --> 17:50.845
But there's
nothing wrong with us.

17:50.880 --> 17:53.247
You're just lazy,
I'm just excitable;

17:53.283 --> 17:54.582
we're-we're both normal.

17:54.617 --> 17:56.617
Now let's go home.

17:56.653 --> 17:57.985
(grunting)

17:59.656 --> 18:01.689
That man is an imposter!

18:01.724 --> 18:04.692
I am George R. R. Martin!

18:04.727 --> 18:06.027
Whoa!

18:09.399 --> 18:11.399
♪ ♪

18:13.903 --> 18:16.037
Let's begin
the Channel Five Cook-Off.

18:16.072 --> 18:18.806
Remember, the winner
gets to keep his TV show.

18:18.841 --> 18:22.310
And the secret ingredient is...

18:22.345 --> 18:23.444
butter.

18:23.479 --> 18:24.745
Aw, sweet!

18:24.781 --> 18:27.181
No. That's
Peter's specialty.

18:30.219 --> 18:31.953
I was hoping
the secret ingredient

18:31.988 --> 18:33.421
would be popcorn shrimps.

18:33.456 --> 18:35.957
I liked smacking
your butt earlier.

18:37.126 --> 18:39.460
Are the balls
different from the cubes?

18:39.495 --> 18:40.962
God, what do I do?

18:40.997 --> 18:42.930
Joe, check if we have shallots!

18:42.966 --> 18:44.565
Okay. I can't see
anything on the counter,

18:44.600 --> 18:45.967
but I'll do my best.

18:47.770 --> 18:49.470
Aah! Monster brains!

18:49.505 --> 18:51.205
TOM: Looks like Chef Griffin
is preparing

18:51.240 --> 18:54.075
a delicious five-course
butter feast.

18:54.110 --> 18:56.010
Son of a bitch,
I never cook with butter.

18:56.045 --> 18:57.979
Joe, grab my recipe book
and find a dish

18:58.014 --> 19:00.081
where I can substitute
olive oil with butter.

19:02.185 --> 19:03.317
Joe, come on, hurry!

19:03.353 --> 19:05.820
I told you
I'm not a strong reader!

19:10.193 --> 19:11.559
Damn it, Joe,
pull it together!

19:11.594 --> 19:14.495
Hey, Quagmire, FYI, I got to
take off in, like, ten minutes.

19:14.530 --> 19:16.230
I got a clarinet lesson.

19:16.265 --> 19:17.598
What?! Ugh.

19:17.633 --> 19:18.866
I can't do this.

19:18.901 --> 19:20.901
(sobbing)

19:22.772 --> 19:25.239
Aw, look at Quagmire.

19:25.274 --> 19:27.141
This was his dream.

19:27.176 --> 19:28.342
Time's almost up, Peter.

19:28.378 --> 19:29.977
We're gonna win!

19:31.881 --> 19:33.881
No, we're not.

19:38.187 --> 19:39.954
TOM: And in a stunning
turn of events,

19:39.989 --> 19:42.056
Chef Griffin
is eating his entire meal.

19:42.091 --> 19:43.591
He'll have nothing to serve.

19:46.129 --> 19:47.595
(buzzer sounds)

19:47.630 --> 19:49.830
(panting)

19:49.866 --> 19:51.599
Well, that's it.
If Chef Quagmire

19:51.634 --> 19:54.969
has prepared a dish of any kind,
he wins this competition.

19:55.004 --> 19:56.370
Let's see what he has.

19:56.406 --> 19:58.839
A warm packet
of tartar sauce

19:58.875 --> 20:00.841
brought in
from somewhere else.

20:00.877 --> 20:03.544
"When are you gonna use that?"
Bonnie said.

20:03.579 --> 20:05.279
We have a winner!

20:05.314 --> 20:06.314
(crowd cheering)

20:08.384 --> 20:11.285
Peter, you threw
the cook-off for me?

20:11.320 --> 20:12.686
I couldn't take
your dream away...

20:12.722 --> 20:13.888
Heart attack!

20:13.923 --> 20:15.456
...from you, buddy.
You're the...

20:15.491 --> 20:17.058
Mini stroke!
...one with the real...

20:17.093 --> 20:18.826
Heart attack!
...talent. You...

20:18.861 --> 20:20.294
Heart attack!
...deserve the show...

20:20.329 --> 20:22.029
Regular stroke!
...not me.

20:22.031 --> 20:24.331
You're a good
friend, Peter.

20:24.367 --> 20:26.967
But... I don't want
a show either.

20:27.003 --> 20:28.035
You don't?

20:28.071 --> 20:29.170
Of course not.

20:29.205 --> 20:31.072
I mean, look what
the show did to us.

20:31.107 --> 20:33.441
It destroyed the most
important friendship I have.

20:33.476 --> 20:34.708
JOE:
Thanks.

20:34.744 --> 20:36.610
You know what?
Let's end this for good.

20:36.646 --> 20:37.812
How?

20:37.847 --> 20:39.046
The same way Paula Deen

20:39.082 --> 20:41.048
got her cooking show
taken off the air.

20:41.084 --> 20:44.018
By saying the one word
you can't come back from.

20:44.053 --> 20:45.152
What?

20:45.188 --> 20:47.388
Say the word? Right now?

20:47.423 --> 20:49.757
Yeah. Together.

20:50.520 --> 20:51.486
Ready?

20:51.521 --> 20:53.054
You bet.
(both inhale)

20:54.890 --> 20:56.857
<i>CLEVELAND: You could have
just quit the show.</i>

20:56.892 --> 20:58.626
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru